subproblem.natural_proof:
Source: parent proof, step 4 (cut-offs), together with the parts of step 5 that concern only the cut-offs: pointwise convergence to 1_K and ∫ a_k → λ(K) = 0. The radii are uniform, ρ_k = 1/(3(k+1)). This is a valid choice of the parent's ρ_x for O_k = thickening(1/(k+1)) K.
Notation: λ := volume on ℝⁿ = Euclid n. For k ∈ ℕ, δ_k := 1/(k+1) > 0 and ρ_k := δ_k/3 > 0.
1. (Finite covers.) Fix k. Since K is compact, there is a finite set T_k ⊆ K with K ⊆ ⋃_{p ∈ T_k} ball(p, ρ_k) (IsCompact.finite_cover_balls); choose such a T_k for every k. Regard T_k as a Finset (Set.Finite.toFinset). If K = ∅, then T_k = ∅.
2. (Bumps.) Euclid n has a HasContDiffBump instance. For p ∈ ℝⁿ, let φ_{k,p} be the smooth bump function (a term of ContDiffBump p) centred at p with inner radius rIn = ρ_k and outer radius rOut = 2ρ_k. This is valid since 0 < ρ_k < 2ρ_k. Its properties:
 - φ_{k,p} is C^∞ (ContDiffBump.contDiff);
 - 0 ≤ φ_{k,p} ≤ 1 (ContDiffBump.nonneg, ContDiffBump.le_one);
 - φ_{k,p}(y) = 1 for y ∈ closedBall(p, ρ_k) (ContDiffBump.one_of_mem_closedBall);
 - φ_{k,p}(y) = 0 whenever dist(y, p) ≥ 2ρ_k (ContDiffBump.zero_of_le_dist; equivalently, support φ_{k,p} = ball(p, 2ρ_k) by ContDiffBump.support_eq).
3. (Definition.) a_k(y) := 1 − P_k(y), where P_k(y) := ∏_{p ∈ T_k} (1 − φ_{k,p}(y)).
4. (Smoothness and range.) Each factor y ↦ 1 − φ_{k,p}(y) is C^∞, and a finite product of C^∞ functions is C^∞ (contDiff_prod). So a_k is C^∞. For every y, each factor lies in [0, 1], so 0 ≤ P_k(y) ≤ 1 (Finset.prod_nonneg, Finset.prod_le_one₀). Hence a_k(y) = 1 − P_k(y) ∈ [0, 1].
5. (a_k = 1 on K.) Let y ∈ K. By step 1, y ∈ ball(p, ρ_k) ⊆ closedBall(p, ρ_k) for some p ∈ T_k (ball_subset_closedBall). Then φ_{k,p}(y) = 1, so the factor 1 − φ_{k,p}(y) vanishes. Hence P_k(y) = 0 (Finset.prod_eq_zero) and a_k(y) = 1.
6. (Support.) Let y ∉ cthickening(δ_k) K and p ∈ T_k. Since p ∈ K, dist(y, p) ≤ δ_k would put y in cthickening(δ_k) K (Metric.mem_cthickening_of_dist_le). So dist(y, p) > δ_k ≥ 2ρ_k, and φ_{k,p}(y) = 0. Hence every factor equals 1, so P_k(y) = 1 (Finset.prod_eq_one) and a_k(y) = 0. Thus a_k vanishes outside cthickening(δ_k) K. That set is compact (IsCompact.cthickening, ℝⁿ being proper), so a_k has compact support (HasCompactSupport.intro).
7. (Eventual vanishing off K.) Let y ∉ K. K is compact, hence closed, so K = closure K = ⋂_{δ>0} cthickening(δ) K (Metric.closure_eq_iInter_cthickening). Hence there is δ > 0 with y ∉ cthickening(δ) K. Choose k₀ ∈ ℕ with 1/(k₀+1) < δ (exists_nat_one_div_lt). For k ≥ k₀, δ_k = 1/(k+1) ≤ 1/(k₀+1) < δ (one_div_le_one_div_of_le). So cthickening(δ_k) K ⊆ cthickening(δ) K (Metric.cthickening_mono), and this set does not contain y. By step 6, a_k(y) = 0 for all k ≥ k₀.
8. (Integrals.) The set C_k := cthickening(δ_k) K is closed, hence measurable. It is compact, hence of finite measure (IsCompact.measure_lt_top). By steps 4 and 6, 0 ≤ a_k ≤ 1_{C_k} pointwise, and 1_{C_k} is integrable (integrableOn_const, IntegrableOn.integrable_indicator). Hence 0 ≤ ∫ a_k dλ (integral_nonneg), and ∫ a_k dλ ≤ ∫ 1_{C_k} dλ = λ(C_k).toReal (integral_mono_of_nonneg, integral_indicator_const, measureReal_def).
 By tendsto_measure_cthickening_of_isCompact (λ is finite on compact sets), λ(cthickening(δ) K) → λ(K) = 0 as δ → 0. Since δ_k → 0 (tendsto_one_div_add_atTop_nhds_zero_nat), λ(C_k) → 0 in [0, ∞]. Then λ(C_k).toReal → 0, since ENNReal.toReal is continuous at 0 (ENNReal.tendsto_toReal). By the squeeze theorem (tendsto_of_tendsto_of_tendsto_of_le_of_le), ∫ a_k dλ → 0.
9. (Conclusion.) The family (a_k) has every required property:
 - C^∞ (step 4);
 - compact support (step 6);
 - 0 ≤ a_k ≤ 1 (step 4);
 - a_k = 1 on K (step 5);
 - eventual vanishing off K (step 7);
 - ∫ a_k dλ → 0 (step 8).
 No case distinction is needed: if K = ∅, then T_k = ∅, P_k = 1 and a_k = 0. Dimension n = 0 is not special.
Remark (equivalent shortcut for steps 1–6). exists_contDiff_support_eq_eq_one_iff is in the import closure. Apply it, with smoothness order ⊤, to the open set s = thickening(δ_k) K and the closed set t = K ⊆ s (self_subset_thickening). It directly gives a C^∞ function a_k with:
 - range in [0, 1];
 - support a_k = thickening(δ_k) K ⊆ cthickening(δ_k) K (thickening_subset_cthickening);
 - a_k(y) = 1 exactly for y ∈ K.
 Then a_k vanishes outside cthickening(δ_k) K, and the compact-support argument of step 6 and steps 7–9 apply unchanged.

